            <ArticleTitle>[Massive localized lymphedema].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>356-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.annder.2015.02.005</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0151-9638(15)00069-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Massive localized lymphedema (MLL) is a benign soft-tissue lesion that usually presents as a large and isolated mass in morbidly obese adults.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report the case of a 39-year-old woman presenting obesity and multiple MLL. There was a large tumor in the left groin and two smaller lesions on the backs of the thighs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">MLL is a benign tumor that must be removed wherever possible because such tumors may degenerate into angiosarcomas in 13% of cases. MLL is probably secondary to a prolonged obstruction of lymphatic vessels due to marked excess of adipose tissue.</AbstractText>
